IMPORT prelude
IMPORT `ccc-core`
IMPORT `ccc20-part16-interpretation`

-- ccc20-part16-arrest.l4 -- sections 494 to 499 of the Criminal Code (Canada): arrest without warrant, and release from custody. Layer 1: the law, objectively.
-- Source: a plain-text rendering of the Justice Laws Website consolidation (current to 2026-09-30); not the official text; nothing is claimed verified against the official text.
-- An answer is JUST TRUE; JUST FALSE (the text says no in its own words: a "shall not", a flat negative, a condition the text introduces with "if", "where", "unless" or a stated period); or
-- NOTHING (not covered: a "may" whose "if" gate fails, a list the text does not close, a term it leaves undefined, an answer taken from a section outside this range that is itself not covered).
-- A function named for a power ("may") answers NOTHING, not JUST FALSE, when its "if" gate fails (ruling of 2026-11-12); a duty, a bar and the provision's own question answer JUST FALSE.
-- Seams: `s 469 ...` (the offence charged is one listed in s 469) and `s 553 ...` (the offence charged is an indictable offence mentioned in s 553) are answered by other parts: MAYBE BOOLEAN inputs.

§ `Section 494 -- Arrest without warrant by any person`

-- 494 (1) Any one may arrest without warrant (a) a person whom he finds committing an indictable offence; or (b) a person who, on reasonable grounds, he believes (i) has committed a criminal offence,
-- and (ii) is escaping from and freshly pursued by persons who have lawful authority to arrest that person. Limb (a) calls s 493.01 for "an indictable offence".
-- src:11074 | Arrest without Warrant and Release from Custody
-- src:11075 | Arrest without warrant by any person
-- src:11076 | 494 (1) Any one may arrest without warrant
-- src:11077 | (a) a person whom he finds committing an indictable offence; or
-- src:11078 | (b) a person who, on reasonable grounds, he believes
-- src:11079 | (i) has committed a criminal offence, and
-- src:11080 | (ii) is escaping from and freshly pursued by persons who have lawful authority to arrest that person.
-- src:11081 | Arrest by owner, etc., of property
@ref s 494(1) (src:11074-11081)
GIVEN offence IS A `s 493.01 the offence charged`
      `the arrester finds the person committing the offence` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the arrester believes on reasonable grounds that the person has committed a criminal offence` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the arrester believes that the person is escaping from and freshly pursued by persons who have lawful authority to arrest` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 494(1) any one may arrest the person without warrant` MEANS
    IF `s 494(1)(a) limb` OR `s 494(1)(b) limb`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE NOTHING   -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): "Any one may arrest without warrant" (a "may" whose limbs (a) and (b) are not met)
    WHERE
        `s 494(1)(a) limb` MEANS `the arrester finds the person committing the offence` AND `s 493.01 the offence is treated as an indictable offence` offence
        `s 494(1)(b) limb` MEANS `the arrester believes on reasonable grounds that the person has committed a criminal offence`
                                 AND `the arrester believes that the person is escaping from and freshly pursued by persons who have lawful authority to arrest`

-- 494 (2) The owner or a person in lawful possession of property, or a person authorized by the owner or by a person in lawful possession of property, may arrest a person without a warrant if they find
-- them committing a criminal offence on or in relation to that property and (a) they make the arrest at that time; or (b) they make the arrest within a reasonable time after the offence is committed and
-- they believe on reasonable grounds that it is not feasible in the circumstances for a peace officer to make the arrest.
-- src:11082 | (2) The owner or a person in lawful possession of property, or a person authorized by the owner or by a person in lawful possession of property, may arrest a person without a warrant if they find them committing a criminal offence on or in relation to that property and
-- src:11083 | (a) they make the arrest at that time; or
-- src:11084 | (b) they make the arrest within a reasonable time after the offence is committed and they believe on reasonable grounds that it is not feasible in the circumstances for a peace officer to make the arrest.
@ref s 494(2) (src:11082-11084)
GIVEN `the arrester is the owner or a person in lawful possession of the property, or a person authorized by one of them` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the arrester finds the person committing a criminal offence on or in relation to the property` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the arrest is made at that time` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the arrest is made within a reasonable time after the offence is committed` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the arrester believes on reasonable grounds that it is not feasible in the circumstances for a peace officer to make the arrest` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 494(2) the owner of property, or a person acting for the owner, may arrest the person without a warrant` MEANS
    IF `the arrester is the owner or a person in lawful possession of the property, or a person authorized by one of them`
       AND `the arrester finds the person committing a criminal offence on or in relation to the property`
       AND (   `the arrest is made at that time`                                                                                    -- (a)
            OR (   `the arrest is made within a reasonable time after the offence is committed`                                       -- (b)
               AND `the arrester believes on reasonable grounds that it is not feasible in the circumstances for a peace officer to make the arrest`))
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE NOTHING   -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): "may arrest a person without a warrant if they find them committing a criminal offence on or in relation to that property" (a "may" whose "if" gate fails)

-- 494 (3) Any one other than a peace officer who arrests a person without warrant shall forthwith deliver the person to a peace officer. A duty: it does not arise for a peace officer or for an arrest with a warrant.
-- src:11085 | Delivery to peace officer
-- src:11086 | (3) Any one other than a peace officer who arrests a person without warrant shall forthwith deliver the person to a peace officer.
@ref s 494(3) (src:11085-11086)
GIVEN `the arrester is a peace officer` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the arrest was made without warrant` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 494(3) the arrester must forthwith deliver the person to a peace officer` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF `the arrester is a peace officer` THEN JUST FALSE   -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "Any one other than a peace officer who arrests a person without warrant shall"
        IF NOT `the arrest was made without warrant` THEN JUST FALSE   -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "who arrests a person without warrant shall"
        OTHERWISE JUST TRUE

-- The duty as a regulative rule. The core's Party has no constructor for a private arrester ("any one other than a peace officer"), so this rule uses a local actor type (NEEDS-CORE.md).
-- "forthwith" states no number of hours or minutes, so the rule has no WITHIN: the residual after no act is the duty itself, not a breach.
@ref s 494(3) the duty as a regulative rule (src:11086)
DECLARE `s 494(3) the actor` IS ONE OF
    `s 494(3) the person who arrested without warrant, other than a peace officer`
    `s 494(3) the peace officer to whom the person is delivered`

DECLARE `s 494(3) the act` IS ONE OF
    `s 494(3) deliver the person to a peace officer`

@ref s 494(3) the duty as a regulative rule (src:11086)
GIVEN `the answer of s 494(3)` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN
GIVETH A DEONTIC `s 494(3) the actor` `s 494(3) the act`
`s 494(3) the delivery duty` MEANS
    IF `the answer of s 494(3)` EQUALS JUST TRUE
    THEN PARTY `s 494(3) the person who arrested without warrant, other than a peace officer`
         MUST `s 494(3) deliver the person to a peace officer`
         HENCE FULFILLED
         LEST BREACH BY `s 494(3) the person who arrested without warrant, other than a peace officer` BECAUSE "the person arrested without warrant was not delivered to a peace officer"
    ELSE FULFILLED

-- 494 (4) For greater certainty, a person who is authorized to make an arrest under this section is a person who is authorized by law to do so for the purposes of section 25.
-- src:11087 | For greater certainty
-- src:11088 | (4) For greater certainty, a person who is authorized to make an arrest under this section is a person who is authorized by law to do so for the purposes of section 25.
@ref s 494(4) (src:11087-11088)
GIVEN `the person is authorized to make an arrest under section 494` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 494(4) the person is authorized by law to make the arrest for the purposes of section 25` MEANS
    IF `the person is authorized to make an arrest under section 494`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE NOTHING   -- NOTHING-kind (silent): subsection (4) says only that a person authorized under section 494 is authorized by law for section 25; it says nothing of any other person

§ `Section 495 -- Arrest without warrant by peace officer`

-- 495 (1) A peace officer may arrest without warrant (a) a person who has committed an indictable offence or who, on reasonable grounds, he believes has committed or is about to commit an indictable
-- offence; (b) a person whom he finds committing a criminal offence; or (c) a person in respect of whom he has reasonable grounds to believe that a warrant of arrest or committal ... is in force within the
-- territorial jurisdiction in which the person is found.
-- src:11089 | Arrest without warrant by peace officer
-- src:11090 | 495 (1) A peace officer may arrest without warrant
-- src:11091 | (a) a person who has committed an indictable offence or who, on reasonable grounds, he believes has committed or is about to commit an indictable offence;
-- src:11092 | (b) a person whom he finds committing a criminal offence; or
-- src:11093 | (c) a person in respect of whom he has reasonable grounds to believe that a warrant of arrest or committal, in any form set out in Part XXVIII in relation thereto, is in force within the territorial jurisdiction in which the person is found.
@ref s 495(1) (src:11089-11093)
GIVEN offence IS A `s 493.01 the offence charged`
      `the person has committed the offence, or the officer believes on reasonable grounds that the person has committed or is about to commit it` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the officer finds the person committing a criminal offence` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the officer has reasonable grounds to believe that a warrant of arrest or committal is in force within the territorial jurisdiction in which the person is found` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 495(1) the peace officer may arrest the person without warrant` MEANS
    IF `s 495(1)(a) limb` OR `s 495(1)(b) limb` OR `s 495(1)(c) limb`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE NOTHING   -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): "A peace officer may arrest without warrant" (a "may" whose limbs (a), (b) and (c) are not met)
    WHERE
        `s 495(1)(a) limb` MEANS `the person has committed the offence, or the officer believes on reasonable grounds that the person has committed or is about to commit it`
                                 AND `s 493.01 the offence is treated as an indictable offence` offence
        `s 495(1)(b) limb` MEANS `the officer finds the person committing a criminal offence`
        `s 495(1)(c) limb` MEANS `the officer has reasonable grounds to believe that a warrant of arrest or committal is in force within the territorial jurisdiction in which the person is found`

-- 495 (2) A peace officer shall not arrest a person without warrant for (a) an indictable offence mentioned in section 553, (b) an offence for which the person may be prosecuted by indictment or for
-- which he is punishable on summary conviction, or (c) an offence punishable on summary conviction, in any case where (d) he believes on reasonable grounds that the public interest, having regard to
-- all the circumstances including the need to (i) establish the identity of the person, (ii) secure or preserve evidence of or relating to the offence, or (iii) prevent the continuation or repetition of
-- the offence or the commission of another offence, may be satisfied without so arresting the person, and (e) he has no reasonable grounds to believe that, if he does not so arrest the person, the
-- person will fail to attend court in order to be dealt with according to law. A bar. Paragraph (b) is the offence that is both; paragraph (c) the summary-only offence; (a) an indictable offence of s 553.
-- src:11094 | Limitation
-- src:11095 | (2) A peace officer shall not arrest a person without warrant for
-- src:11096 | (a) an indictable offence mentioned in section 553,
-- src:11097 | (b) an offence for which the person may be prosecuted by indictment or for which he is punishable on summary conviction, or
-- src:11098 | (c) an offence punishable on summary conviction,
-- src:11099 | in any case where
-- src:11100 | (d) he believes on reasonable grounds that the public interest, having regard to all the circumstances including the need to
-- src:11101 | (i) establish the identity of the person,
-- src:11102 | (ii) secure or preserve evidence of or relating to the offence, or
-- src:11103 | (iii) prevent the continuation or repetition of the offence or the commission of another offence,
-- src:11104 | may be satisfied without so arresting the person, and
-- src:11105 | (e) he has no reasonable grounds to believe that, if he does not so arrest the person, the person will fail to attend court in order to be dealt with according to law.
@ref s 495(2) (src:11094-11105)
DECLARE `s 495(2)(d) a circumstance the text names` IS ONE OF
    `s 495(2)(d)(i) the need to establish the identity of the person`
    `s 495(2)(d)(ii) the need to secure or preserve evidence of or relating to the offence`
    `s 495(2)(d)(iii) the need to prevent the continuation or repetition of the offence or the commission of another offence`
    `s 495(2)(d) a circumstance the text does not name`

@ref s 495(2)(d)(i)-(iii) (src:11101-11104)
GIVEN circumstance IS A `s 495(2)(d) a circumstance the text names`
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 495(2)(d) the circumstance is a need the text names` MEANS
    CONSIDER circumstance
    WHEN `s 495(2)(d)(i) the need to establish the identity of the person` THEN JUST TRUE
    WHEN `s 495(2)(d)(ii) the need to secure or preserve evidence of or relating to the offence` THEN JUST TRUE
    WHEN `s 495(2)(d)(iii) the need to prevent the continuation or repetition of the offence or the commission of another offence` THEN JUST TRUE
    WHEN `s 495(2)(d) a circumstance the text does not name` THEN NOTHING   -- NOTHING-kind (includes): "having regard to all the circumstances including the need to"

@ref s 495(2) (src:11094-11105)
GIVEN class IS A `Class of offence`
      `s 553 the offence charged is an indictable offence mentioned in section 553` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN
      `the officer believes on reasonable grounds that the public interest may be satisfied without so arresting the person` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the officer has no reasonable grounds to believe that, if he does not so arrest the person, the person will fail to attend court` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 495(2) the peace officer is barred from arresting the person without warrant` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF class EQUALS `an indictable offence` AND `s 553 the offence charged is an indictable offence mentioned in section 553` EQUALS NOTHING
            THEN NOTHING   -- NOTHING-kind (points outside the text): "(a) an indictable offence mentioned in section 553" (section 553 is outside this range)
        IF class EQUALS `an indictable offence` AND `s 553 the offence charged is an indictable offence mentioned in section 553` EQUALS JUST FALSE
            THEN NOTHING   -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): "shall not arrest a person without warrant for (a) an indictable offence mentioned in section 553" (an indictable offence not mentioned there is outside the bar's three limbs)
        IF NOT `the officer believes on reasonable grounds that the public interest may be satisfied without so arresting the person`
            THEN JUST FALSE   -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "in any case where (d) he believes on reasonable grounds that the public interest"
        IF NOT `the officer has no reasonable grounds to believe that, if he does not so arrest the person, the person will fail to attend court`
            THEN JUST FALSE   -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "(e) he has no reasonable grounds to believe that, if he does not so arrest the person, the person will fail to attend court"
        OTHERWISE JUST TRUE

-- 495 (3) Notwithstanding subsection (2), a peace officer acting under subsection (1) is deemed to be acting lawfully and in the execution of his duty for the purposes of (a) any proceedings under this
-- or any other Act of Parliament; and (b) any other proceedings, unless in any such proceedings it is alleged and established by the person making the allegation that the peace officer did not comply
-- with the requirements of subsection (2).
-- src:11106 | Consequences of arrest without warrant
-- src:11107 | (3) Notwithstanding subsection (2), a peace officer acting under subsection (1) is deemed to be acting lawfully and in the execution of his duty for the purposes of
-- src:11108 | (a) any proceedings under this or any other Act of Parliament; and
-- src:11109 | (b) any other proceedings, unless in any such proceedings it is alleged and established by the person making the allegation that the peace officer did not comply with the requirements of subsection (2).
@ref s 495(3) (src:11106-11109)
GIVEN `the peace officer was acting under subsection (1)` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the proceedings are under this or any other Act of Parliament` IS A BOOLEAN
      `it is alleged and established by the person making the allegation that the peace officer did not comply with subsection (2)` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 495(3) the peace officer is deemed to be acting lawfully and in the execution of their duty` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF NOT `the peace officer was acting under subsection (1)` THEN NOTHING   -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): "a peace officer acting under subsection (1) is deemed"
        IF `the proceedings are under this or any other Act of Parliament` THEN JUST TRUE   -- paragraph (a): no exception is stated
        IF `it is alleged and established by the person making the allegation that the peace officer did not comply with subsection (2)` THEN JUST FALSE   -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "(b) any other proceedings, unless in any such proceedings it is alleged and established"
        OTHERWISE JUST TRUE   -- paragraph (b)

-- 495.1 Despite any other provision in this Act, if a peace officer has reasonable grounds to believe that an accused has contravened or is about to contravene a summons, appearance notice, undertaking or
-- release order ..., or has committed an offence while being subject to a summons, appearance notice, undertaking or release order, the peace officer may arrest the accused without a warrant for the
-- purpose of taking them before a judge or justice to be dealt with under section 524.
-- src:11110 | Arrest without warrant — application of section 524
-- src:11111 | 495.1 Despite any other provision in this Act, if a peace officer has reasonable grounds to believe that an accused has contravened or is about to contravene a summons, appearance notice, undertaking or release order that was issued or given to the accused or entered into by the accused, or has committed an offence while being subject to a summons, appearance notice, undertaking or release order, the peace officer may arrest the accused without a warrant for the purpose of taking them before a judge or justice to be dealt with under section 524.
@ref s 495.1 (src:11110-11111)
GIVEN `the officer has reasonable grounds to believe that the accused has contravened or is about to contravene a summons, appearance notice, undertaking or release order` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the officer has reasonable grounds to believe that the accused has committed an offence while subject to a summons, appearance notice, undertaking or release order` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 495.1 the peace officer may arrest the accused without a warrant to take them before a judge or justice under section 524` MEANS
    IF `the officer has reasonable grounds to believe that the accused has contravened or is about to contravene a summons, appearance notice, undertaking or release order`
       OR `the officer has reasonable grounds to believe that the accused has committed an offence while subject to a summons, appearance notice, undertaking or release order`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE NOTHING   -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): "if a peace officer has reasonable grounds to believe" (a "may" whose "if" gate fails)

§ `Sections 496 and 497 -- Appearance notices issued by a peace officer`

-- 496 If a peace officer has reasonable grounds to believe that a person has failed to comply with a summons, appearance notice, undertaking or release order or to attend court as required and that the
-- failure did not cause a victim physical or emotional harm, property damage or economic loss, the peace officer may, without laying a charge, issue an appearance notice to the person to appear at a
-- judicial referral hearing under section 523.1.
-- src:11112 | Appearance notice for judicial referral hearing
-- src:11113 | 496 If a peace officer has reasonable grounds to believe that a person has failed to comply with a summons, appearance notice, undertaking or release order or to attend court as required and that the failure did not cause a victim physical or emotional harm, property damage or economic loss, the peace officer may, without laying a charge, issue an appearance notice to the person to appear at a judicial referral hearing under section 523.1.
@ref s 496 (src:11112-11113)
GIVEN `the officer has reasonable grounds to believe that the person has failed to comply with a summons, appearance notice, undertaking or release order or to attend court as required` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the officer has reasonable grounds to believe that the failure did not cause a victim physical or emotional harm, property damage or economic loss` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 496 the peace officer may, without laying a charge, issue an appearance notice to attend a judicial referral hearing` MEANS
    IF `the officer has reasonable grounds to believe that the person has failed to comply with a summons, appearance notice, undertaking or release order or to attend court as required`
       AND `the officer has reasonable grounds to believe that the failure did not cause a victim physical or emotional harm, property damage or economic loss`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE NOTHING   -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): "If a peace officer has reasonable grounds to believe" (a "may" whose "if" gate fails)

-- 497 If, by virtue of subsection 495(2), a peace officer does not arrest a person, they may issue an appearance notice to the person if the offence is (a) an indictable offence mentioned in section 553;
-- (b) an offence for which the person may be prosecuted by indictment or for which they are punishable on summary conviction; or (c) an offence punishable on summary conviction.
-- The bar of s 495(2) is taken as the answer of `s 495(2)` (a function of this module); the three paragraphs are the same three as 495(2)(a), (b), (c).
-- src:11114 | Issue of appearance notice by peace officer
-- src:11115 | 497 If, by virtue of subsection 495(2), a peace officer does not arrest a person, they may issue an appearance notice to the person if the offence is
-- src:11116 | (a) an indictable offence mentioned in section 553;
-- src:11117 | (b) an offence for which the person may be prosecuted by indictment or for which they are punishable on summary conviction; or
-- src:11118 | (c) an offence punishable on summary conviction.
@ref s 497 (src:11114-11118)
GIVEN `the answer of s 495(2)` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN
      class IS A `Class of offence`
      `s 553 the offence charged is an indictable offence mentioned in section 553` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 497 the peace officer may issue an appearance notice to the person` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF `the answer of s 495(2)` EQUALS NOTHING THEN NOTHING   -- NOTHING-kind (points outside the text): "If, by virtue of subsection 495(2)" (the answer of subsection 495(2) is itself not covered)
        IF `the answer of s 495(2)` EQUALS JUST FALSE THEN NOTHING   -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): "If, by virtue of subsection 495(2), a peace officer does not arrest a person, they may issue" (a "may" whose "if" gate fails)
        IF class EQUALS `an indictable offence` AND `s 553 the offence charged is an indictable offence mentioned in section 553` EQUALS NOTHING
            THEN NOTHING   -- NOTHING-kind (points outside the text): "(a) an indictable offence mentioned in section 553" (section 553 is outside this range)
        IF class EQUALS `an indictable offence` AND `s 553 the offence charged is an indictable offence mentioned in section 553` EQUALS JUST FALSE
            THEN NOTHING   -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): "if the offence is (a) an indictable offence mentioned in section 553" (an indictable offence not mentioned there is not one of (a), (b), (c))
        OTHERWISE JUST TRUE   -- the offence is one of (a), (b) or (c)

§ `Section 498 -- Release from custody: arrest without warrant`

-- 498 (1) Subject to subsection (1.1), if a person has been arrested without warrant for an offence, other than one listed in section 469, and has not been taken before a justice or released from
-- custody under any other provision of this Part, a peace officer shall, as soon as practicable, release the person, if (a) the peace officer intends to compel the person's appearance by way of summons;
-- (b) the peace officer issues an appearance notice to the person; or (c) the person gives an undertaking to the peace officer. Subsection (1.01) extends (1) to a person delivered to a peace officer
-- under 494(3) or placed in custody under s 163.5(3) of the Customs Act. Subsection (2): (1) and (1.01) do not apply to an offence described in subsection 503(3).
-- src:11119 | Release from custody — arrest without warrant
-- src:11120 | 498 (1) Subject to subsection (1.1), if a person has been arrested without warrant for an offence, other than one listed in section 469, and has not been taken before a justice or released from custody under any other provision of this Part, a peace officer shall, as soon as practicable, release the person, if
-- src:11121 | (a) the peace officer intends to compel the person’s appearance by way of summons;
-- src:11122 | (b) the peace officer issues an appearance notice to the person; or
-- src:11123 | (c) the person gives an undertaking to the peace officer.
-- src:11124 | Person delivered or detained
-- src:11125 | (1.01) Subsection (1) also applies in respect of a person who has been arrested without warrant and delivered to a peace officer under subsection 494(3) or placed in the custody of a peace officer under subsection 163.5(3) of the Customs Act and who is detained in custody for an offence other than one listed in section 469 and who has not been taken before a justice or released from custody under any other provision of this Part.
@ref s 498(1) and (1.01) (src:11119-11125)
DECLARE `s 498(1) how the release is to be effected` IS ONE OF
    `s 498(1)(a) the peace officer intends to compel the person's appearance by way of summons`
    `s 498(1)(b) the peace officer issues an appearance notice to the person`
    `s 498(1)(c) the person gives an undertaking to the peace officer`
    `s 498(1) none of those three`

@ref s 498(1) and (1.01) (src:11119-11125)
GIVEN `the person has been arrested without warrant, or was delivered to a peace officer under subsection 494(3) or placed in the custody of a peace officer under subsection 163.5(3) of the Customs Act` IS A BOOLEAN
      `s 469 the offence charged is one listed in section 469` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN
      `the person has been taken before a justice or released from custody under another provision of this Part` IS A BOOLEAN
      how IS A `s 498(1) how the release is to be effected`
      `the offence is one described in subsection 503(3)` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 498(1) a peace officer must, as soon as practicable, release the person, subject to subsection (1.1)` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF `the offence is one described in subsection 503(3)` THEN JUST FALSE   -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "(2) Subsections (1) and (1.01) do not apply in respect of a person who has been arrested without warrant by a peace officer for an offence described in subsection 503(3)"
        IF NOT `the person has been arrested without warrant, or was delivered to a peace officer under subsection 494(3) or placed in the custody of a peace officer under subsection 163.5(3) of the Customs Act`
            THEN JUST FALSE   -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "if a person has been arrested without warrant for an offence"
        IF `the person has been taken before a justice or released from custody under another provision of this Part` THEN JUST FALSE   -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "and has not been taken before a justice or released from custody under any other provision of this Part"
        IF `s 469 the offence charged is one listed in section 469` EQUALS NOTHING THEN NOTHING   -- NOTHING-kind (points outside the text): "other than one listed in section 469" (section 469 is outside this range)
        IF `s 469 the offence charged is one listed in section 469` EQUALS JUST TRUE THEN JUST FALSE   -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "for an offence, other than one listed in section 469"
        IF how EQUALS `s 498(1) none of those three` THEN JUST FALSE   -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "a peace officer shall, as soon as practicable, release the person, if (a) ... (b) ... or (c)"
        OTHERWISE JUST TRUE

-- The duty of 498(1), as a regulative rule: the officer must release the person, with no time stated beyond "as soon as practicable". The gate is the answer of the two functions above.
@ref s 498(1) the duty as a regulative rule (src:11119-11125)
DECLARE `s 498(1) the act` IS ONE OF
    `s 498(1) release the person`

@ref s 498(1) the duty as a regulative rule (src:11119-11125)
GIVEN `the answer of s 498(1)` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN
      `the answer of s 498(1.1)` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN
GIVETH A DEONTIC Party `s 498(1) the act`
`s 498(1) the release duty` MEANS
    IF `the answer of s 498(1)` EQUALS JUST TRUE AND `the answer of s 498(1.1)` EQUALS JUST FALSE
    THEN PARTY `a peace officer`
         MUST `s 498(1) release the person`
         HENCE FULFILLED
         LEST BREACH BY `a peace officer` BECAUSE "the person was not released as soon as practicable"
    ELSE FULFILLED

-- 498 (1.1) The peace officer shall not release the person if the peace officer believes, on reasonable grounds, (a) that it is necessary in the public interest that the person be detained in custody or that
-- the matter of their release from custody be dealt with under another provision of this Part, having regard to all the circumstances including the need to (i) establish the identity of the person,
-- (ii) secure or preserve evidence of or relating to the offence, (iii) prevent the continuation or repetition of the offence or the commission of another offence, or (iv) ensure the safety and security of any
-- victim of or witness to the offence; or (b) that, if the person is released from custody, the person will fail to attend court in order to be dealt with according to law.
-- src:11126 | Exception
-- src:11127 | (1.1) The peace officer shall not release the person if the peace officer believes, on reasonable grounds,
-- src:11128 | (a) that it is necessary in the public interest that the person be detained in custody or that the matter of their release from custody be dealt with under another provision of this Part, having regard to all the circumstances including the need to
-- src:11129 | (i) establish the identity of the person,
-- src:11130 | (ii) secure or preserve evidence of or relating to the offence,
-- src:11131 | (iii) prevent the continuation or repetition of the offence or the commission of another offence, or
-- src:11132 | (iv) ensure the safety and security of any victim of or witness to the offence; or
-- src:11133 | (b) that, if the person is released from custody, the person will fail to attend court in order to be dealt with according to law.
@ref s 498(1.1) (src:11126-11133)
DECLARE `s 498(1.1)(a) a circumstance the text names` IS ONE OF
    `s 498(1.1)(a)(i) the need to establish the identity of the person`
    `s 498(1.1)(a)(ii) the need to secure or preserve evidence of or relating to the offence`
    `s 498(1.1)(a)(iii) the need to prevent the continuation or repetition of the offence or the commission of another offence`
    `s 498(1.1)(a)(iv) the need to ensure the safety and security of any victim of or witness to the offence`
    `s 498(1.1)(a) a circumstance the text does not name`

@ref s 498(1.1)(a)(i)-(iv) (src:11129-11132)
GIVEN circumstance IS A `s 498(1.1)(a) a circumstance the text names`
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 498(1.1)(a) the circumstance is a need the text names` MEANS
    CONSIDER circumstance
    WHEN `s 498(1.1)(a)(i) the need to establish the identity of the person` THEN JUST TRUE
    WHEN `s 498(1.1)(a)(ii) the need to secure or preserve evidence of or relating to the offence` THEN JUST TRUE
    WHEN `s 498(1.1)(a)(iii) the need to prevent the continuation or repetition of the offence or the commission of another offence` THEN JUST TRUE
    WHEN `s 498(1.1)(a)(iv) the need to ensure the safety and security of any victim of or witness to the offence` THEN JUST TRUE
    WHEN `s 498(1.1)(a) a circumstance the text does not name` THEN NOTHING   -- NOTHING-kind (includes): "having regard to all the circumstances including the need to"

@ref s 498(1.1) (src:11126-11133)
GIVEN `the peace officer believes on reasonable grounds that it is necessary in the public interest that the person be detained in custody or that the matter of their release be dealt with under another provision of this Part` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the peace officer believes on reasonable grounds that, if the person is released from custody, the person will fail to attend court` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 498(1.1) the peace officer must not release the person` MEANS
    IF `the peace officer believes on reasonable grounds that it is necessary in the public interest that the person be detained in custody or that the matter of their release be dealt with under another provision of this Part`   -- (a)
       OR `the peace officer believes on reasonable grounds that, if the person is released from custody, the person will fail to attend court`   -- (b)
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE JUST FALSE   -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "shall not release the person if the peace officer believes, on reasonable grounds" (neither belief (a) nor (b) is held)

-- 498 (3) A peace officer who has arrested a person without a warrant, or who has been given the custody of a person arrested without a warrant, for an offence described in subsection (1), and who does
-- not release the person from custody as soon as practicable in the manner described in that subsection shall be deemed to be acting lawfully and in the execution of the officer's duty for the purposes of
-- (a) any proceedings under this or any other Act of Parliament; or (b) any other proceedings, unless in any such proceedings it is alleged and established by the person making the allegation that the peace
-- officer did not comply with the requirements of subsection (1).
-- src:11134 | When subsections (1) and (1.01) do not apply
-- src:11135 | (2) Subsections (1) and (1.01) do not apply in respect of a person who has been arrested without warrant by a peace officer for an offence described in subsection 503(3).
-- src:11136 | Consequences of non-release
-- src:11137 | (3) A peace officer who has arrested a person without a warrant, or who has been given the custody of a person arrested without a warrant, for an offence described in subsection (1), and who does not release the person from custody as soon as practicable in the manner described in that subsection shall be deemed to be acting lawfully and in the execution of the officer’s duty for the purposes of
-- src:11138 | (a) any proceedings under this or any other Act of Parliament; or
-- src:11139 | (b) any other proceedings, unless in any such proceedings it is alleged and established by the person making the allegation that the peace officer did not comply with the requirements of subsection (1).
@ref s 498(2) and (3) (src:11134-11139)
GIVEN `the officer arrested the person without a warrant, or was given the custody of a person arrested without a warrant, for an offence described in subsection (1), and did not release the person as soon as practicable in the manner described in that subsection` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the proceedings are under this or any other Act of Parliament` IS A BOOLEAN
      `it is alleged and established by the person making the allegation that the peace officer did not comply with subsection (1)` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 498(3) the peace officer is deemed to be acting lawfully and in the execution of the officer's duty` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF NOT `the officer arrested the person without a warrant, or was given the custody of a person arrested without a warrant, for an offence described in subsection (1), and did not release the person as soon as practicable in the manner described in that subsection`
            THEN NOTHING   -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): "A peace officer who has arrested a person without a warrant, or who has been given the custody of a person arrested without a warrant, for an offence described in subsection (1), and who does not release the person"
        IF `the proceedings are under this or any other Act of Parliament` THEN JUST TRUE   -- paragraph (a): no exception is stated
        IF `it is alleged and established by the person making the allegation that the peace officer did not comply with subsection (1)` THEN JUST FALSE   -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "(b) any other proceedings, unless in any such proceedings it is alleged and established"
        OTHERWISE JUST TRUE   -- paragraph (b)

§ `Section 499 -- Release from custody: arrest with warrant`

-- 499 If a person who has been arrested with a warrant by a peace officer is taken into custody for an offence other than one listed in section 469 and the warrant has been endorsed by a justice under
-- subsection 507(6), a peace officer may release the person, if (a) the peace officer issues an appearance notice to the person; or (b) the person gives an undertaking to the peace officer.
-- src:11140 | Release from custody — arrest with warrant
-- src:11141 | 499 If a person who has been arrested with a warrant by a peace officer is taken into custody for an offence other than one listed in section 469 and the warrant has been endorsed by a justice under subsection 507(6), a peace officer may release the person, if
-- src:11142 | (a) the peace officer issues an appearance notice to the person; or
-- src:11143 | (b) the person gives an undertaking to the peace officer.
@ref s 499 (src:11140-11143)
GIVEN `the person was arrested with a warrant by a peace officer and taken into custody` IS A BOOLEAN
      `s 469 the offence charged is one listed in section 469` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN
      `the warrant has been endorsed by a justice under subsection 507(6)` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the peace officer issues an appearance notice to the person` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the person gives an undertaking to the peace officer` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 499 a peace officer may release the person arrested with a warrant` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF `s 469 the offence charged is one listed in section 469` EQUALS NOTHING THEN NOTHING   -- NOTHING-kind (points outside the text): "for an offence other than one listed in section 469" (section 469 is outside this range)
        IF NOT `the person was arrested with a warrant by a peace officer and taken into custody` THEN NOTHING   -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): "If a person who has been arrested with a warrant by a peace officer is taken into custody" (a "may" whose "if" gate fails)
        IF `s 469 the offence charged is one listed in section 469` EQUALS JUST TRUE THEN NOTHING   -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): "for an offence other than one listed in section 469" (a "may" whose "if" gate fails)
        IF NOT `the warrant has been endorsed by a justice under subsection 507(6)` THEN NOTHING   -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): "and the warrant has been endorsed by a justice under subsection 507(6)" (a "may" whose "if" gate fails)
        IF `the peace officer issues an appearance notice to the person` OR `the person gives an undertaking to the peace officer` THEN JUST TRUE   -- (a) or (b)
        OTHERWISE NOTHING   -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): "a peace officer may release the person, if (a) ... or (b) ..." (a "may" whose "if" gate fails)
